{"id": "E4", "kind": "learn", "question": "Does the log2 law y = 6.31*log2(x) - 7.80 hold at small positive x (below 1), and is the boundary at exactly x=0 or somewhere else near 0?", "expect": "If the log law is exact, y at 0.001 \u2248 6.31*(-9.966)-7.80 \u2248 -70.7, at 0.1 \u2248 -28.76, at 0.5 \u2248 -14.11, at 0.9 \u2248 -8.76, at 1.0 \u2248 -7.80, at 2.0 \u2248 -1.49, at 3.0 \u2248 2.20, at 4.0 \u2248 4.82, at 7.0 \u2248 9.91. If instead the function saturates or switches to a different branch for small x (e.g. a floor, or a linear segment), the small-x values will deviate from the log prediction \u2014 that would reveal a sub-interval. Values at 0.0 and negatives should be null if the domain is x>0; if 0.0 or small negatives return numbers, the boundary is different.", "probe": {"x": [0.001, 0.01, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0, 0.0, -0.001, -0.01, -0.1, -1.0, 7.0]}, "observation": {"y": [-70.6769, -49.7179, -35.0682, -28.759, -22.4497, -18.759, -14.1093, -11.0466, -8.759, -7.8, -4.1093, -1.4907, 2.2, 4.8186, null, null, null, null, null, 9.9124]}, "model_version": 1, "model_prediction": {"y": [-70.68409883621777, -49.72273255747851, -35.071366278739255, -28.761366278739256, -22.451366278739254, -18.76025289918876, -14.11, -11.046956720555775, -8.759139519638264, -7.8, -4.108886620449505, -1.4900000000000002, 2.2011133795504945, 4.819999999999999, null, null, null, null, null, 9.914409558183479]}, "match": 1.0, "model_error": null}
