{"id": "E6", "kind": "learn", "question": "Is the model's log2 law exact at typical mid-range positive x values (fractional, non-power-of-two), and does it hold for x between 0 and 1 that we haven't densely sampled?", "expect": "If the log2 law y=6.31*log2(x)-7.80 is exact, predictions match to ~4 decimals across all these ordinary fractional values. Any systematic deviation (e.g., in the sub-1 region or at particular magnitudes) would reveal a piecewise structure or a different functional form (e.g., a genuine log with a different base/offset, or a linear-plus-log combination) that the current model misses.", "probe": {"x": [12.5, 17.3, 23.7, 33.3, 45.6, 55.5, 63.1, 71.4, 85.5, 93.2, 111.1, 125.0, 0.15, 0.25, 0.45, 0.6, 0.8, 1.3, 2.5, 3.7]}, "observation": {"y": [15.1901, 18.1482, 21.0133, 24.1089, 26.9702, 28.7586, 29.9268, 31.0516, 32.692, 33.4769, 35.0761, 36.1491, -25.0683, -20.4186, -15.0683, -12.4497, -9.8311, -5.4119, 0.5404, 4.1089]}, "model_version": 1, "model_prediction": {"y": [15.19273255747851, 18.15113783764847, 21.016603621808756, 24.112511217480403, 26.974169710640265, 28.762764116669164, 29.931074711600626, 31.05604718728436, 32.69664936893002, 33.481648294029256, 35.08096169102591, 36.15409883621777, -25.07025289918876, -20.419999999999998, -15.069139519638265, -12.450252899188762, -9.831366278739257, -5.411591657268964, 0.5413662787392566, 4.110284458379417]}, "match": 1.0, "model_error": null}
