{"id": "E8", "kind": "learn", "question": "Is the log2 law exact at very large positive x near the upper domain limit (where the model has only one point at 128), and does it hold at the small positive values just above the boundary that were only sparsely sampled?", "expect": "All should match y = 6.31*log2(x) - 7.80. At x=126: ~36.14; 127: ~36.19; 127.5: ~36.21. Small x: 0.02->-43.4, 0.03->-39.9, 0.04->-37.4, 0.06->-33.9, 0.07->-32.6, 0.08->-31.5, 0.12->-27.8, 0.18->-24.0, 0.22->-22.1, 0.35->-17.5, 0.4->-16.4, 0.55->-13.5, 0.65->-11.8, 0.75->-10.3, 0.85->-9.1, 0.95->-8.1, 1.1->-6.9. Any deviation near 128 would reveal a cap/saturation not captured by the pure log; deviation at small x would reveal a floor or different behavior near the boundary.", "probe": {"x": [126.0, 127.0, 127.5, 0.02, 0.03, 0.04, 0.06, 0.07, 0.08, 0.12, 0.18, 0.22, 0.35, 0.4, 0.55, 0.65, 0.75, 0.85, 0.95, 1.1]}, "observation": {"y": [36.2216, 36.2936, 36.3293, -43.4087, -39.718, -37.0994, -33.4087, -32.0056, -30.7901, -27.0994, -23.4087, -21.5821, -17.3559, -16.1404, -13.2417, -11.7211, -10.4186, -9.2793, -8.2669, -6.9325]}, "model_version": 1, "model_prediction": {"y": [36.22663631728447, 36.298600373532366, 36.33437018657939, -43.412732557478506, -39.72161917792801, -37.10273255747851, -33.41161917792801, -32.008322999295025, -30.792732557478512, -27.10161917792802, -23.41050579837752, -21.583719043877167, -17.356956720555775, -16.141366278739255, -13.242352765137909, -11.721591657268963, -10.418886620449504, -9.279475750449615, -8.266943668910232, -6.93235276513791]}, "match": 1.0, "model_error": null}
