{"id": "E10", "kind": "learn", "question": "Does the log2 law hold exactly at the boundary region just above x=0 where the curve is steep, and at the very top of the domain where only one or two points exist \u2014 specifically testing whether the constant offset and slope are exact rather than approximate?", "expect": "If the log2 law y=6.31*log2(x)-7.80 is exact, we get ~ -35.9, -34.0, -32.3, -29.9, -27.1, -23.4, -19.3, -13.2, -3.4, 3.4, 6.31*0-7.80=-7.80... wait, at x=1 y=-7.80. Predictions: 6.31*log2(x)-7.80. Any systematic deviation (curvature, different slope, or offset) would reveal the true form; a perfect match confirms the model and lets us finalize. This densely samples the steep low-x region and the top boundary where errors would be most visible.", "probe": {"x": [0.005, 0.008, 0.011, 0.015, 0.02, 0.03, 0.05, 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0, 10.0, 20.0, 40.0, 64.0, 100.0, 127.9, 128.0]}, "observation": {"y": [-56.0272, -51.7491, -48.8504, -46.0272, -43.4087, -39.718, -35.0682, -28.759, -18.759, -14.1093, -7.8, -1.4907, 6.8497, 13.159, 19.4682, 25.7775, 30.0557, 34.1179, 36.3578, 36.365]}, "model_version": 1, "model_prediction": {"y": [-56.032732557478504, -51.754098836217764, -48.85508532261642, -46.03161917792801, -43.412732557478506, -39.72161917792801, -35.071366278739255, -28.761366278739256, -18.76025289918876, -14.11, -7.8, -1.4900000000000002, 6.851366278739255, 13.161366278739255, 19.471366278739257, 25.781366278739252, 30.06, 34.122732557478514, 36.36288518470387, 36.37]}, "match": 1.0, "model_error": null}
